# Answer 5 · Feminist Movements in Pakistan (CSS 2022)
@py question 2022-6

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note "In detail" rewards a phase-by-phase history with dates, organisations and laws, plus the many actors, and a closing evaluation.
@ask DISCUSS IN DETAIL feminist movements in PAKISTAN
(1) Introduction: collective action for women's rights since 1947; plural movements, not one
(2) Roots: reformers, Muslim women's education and the Pakistan Movement
(3) Welfare and reform, 1947–70: APWA in 1949; inheritance law of 1948; MFLO of 1961
(4) Constitutional equality, 1970s: Articles 25, 27 and 34 of the 1973 Constitution
(5) Resistance under Zia, 1977–88: Hudood laws; WAF in 1981; 12 February 1983; Safia Bibi
(6) Democracy, Beijing and NGOs, 1988–99: Benazir Bhutto; CEDAW 1996; NGO boom
(7) Reforms and machinery, 2000–17: NCSW; 60 seats; laws of 2006, 2010, 2011 and 2016
(8) New activism, 2018 onward: Aurat March; digital campaigns; laws of 2021 and 2025
(9) Other movements: rural women, health workers, religious women, artists and lawyers
(10) Evaluation: strong in law, weak in reach and enforcement
(11) Conclusion: many movements, one long struggle
:::

## Decoding the question
**"Discuss in detail"** asks for a full, organised account — the plural "**movements**" invites you to show that there has been **more than one** movement: elite welfare, secular rights, rural, religious and digital. The strongest structure is **phase by phase** (with dates, organisations, laws and leaders), then a heading on **other actors**, then a brief **evaluation**.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Feminist movements** in Pakistan are the organised, collective efforts of women — and supportive men — to secure women's rights and change their status since ==1947==.

#### ii) Scholarship
**Khawar Mumtaz and Farida Shaheed**'s *Women of Pakistan: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back?* (==1987==) remains the standard history.

#### iii) The argument
Pakistan has had **several movements**, not one: welfare-oriented, rights-based, rural, religious and digital. Together they have changed the **law** substantially, while daily life has changed more slowly.

### 2. Roots before 1947

#### i) Reform
**Sayyid Mumtaz Ali**'s *Huquq-un-Niswan* (==1898==) argued for women's rights from the Qur'an; girls' schools such as Aligarh (==1906==) spread education.

#### ii) The Pakistan Movement
The Muslim League's women's sub-committee (==1938==) drew women into politics under **Fatima Jinnah**, **Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz** and **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan**.

### 3. Welfare and reform (1947–1970)

#### i) Partition relief and APWA
Women's relief work during Partition led to the **All Pakistan Women's Association** (==1949==), founded by **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan** — schools, colleges, clinics and crafts.

#### ii) Inheritance (1948)
Women legislators and demonstrators won the **West Punjab Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act 1948**, giving women inheritance in agricultural land.

#### iii) Family law (1961)
After protests over PM **Bogra**'s second marriage (==1955==) came the **Rashid Commission** and the **Muslim Family Laws Ordinance 1961** — registration of marriage, and restrictions on polygamy and unilateral divorce.

### 4. Constitutional equality (1970s)

The **1973 Constitution** guaranteed equality before law and non-discrimination on the basis of sex (**Article 25**), non-discrimination in public service (**Article 27**) and women's **full participation** in national life (**Article 34**). **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan** became Governor of Sindh (==1973==), and women entered the foreign service and other cadres. For International Women's Year (==1975==) the government set up a committee on women's rights. This was a period of **promises from above** rather than mass mobilisation — the Constitution gave activists a legal standard they would use for decades.

### 5. Resistance under Zia (1977–1988)

#### i) The laws
The **Hudood Ordinances** (==1979==) made it possible for rape victims unable to prove rape to be charged with **zina**. The **Qanun-e-Shahadat** (==1984==) required two women witnesses in some financial matters.

#### ii) WAF
The **Women's Action Forum**, founded in Karachi in ==1981== after the **Fehmida–Allah Bux** stoning sentence, was secular, non-party and non-hierarchical.

#### iii) 12 February 1983
WAF and women lawyers marching in Lahore were **baton-charged**; the day is now **National Women's Day**. The **Safia Bibi** case (==1983==) — a visually impaired rape victim convicted of zina — caused national outrage.

### 6. Democracy, Beijing and NGOs (1988–1999)

#### i) A woman prime minister
**Benazir Bhutto** (==1988==) set up the **First Women Bank** (==1989==), **women's police stations** and the **Lady Health Worker Programme** (==1994==).

#### ii) The UN
Pakistan joined **Beijing** (==1995==) and **acceded to CEDAW** (==1996==).

#### iii) NGOs
**Shirkat Gah** (==1975==), **Aurat Foundation** (==1986==) and many others researched, provided legal aid and lobbied. The **HRCP** (==1987==) documented violence against women, and the killing of **Samia Sarwar** in her lawyer's office (==1999==) brought honour killing to national attention.

#### iv) The limits of the decade
Despite a woman prime minister, the **Hudood laws were not repealed** — a sign of how strong conservative opposition remained.

### 7. Reforms and machinery (2000–2017)

#### i) Institutions
The **NCSW** (==2000==); **33%** local-government seats (==2001==); **60** reserved National Assembly seats (==2002==); the **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** (==2008==).

#### ii) Laws
The **Protection of Women Act 2006** moved rape to the Penal Code; laws on **workplace harassment** (==2010==), **acid attacks** and **anti-women practices** (==2011==), and **honour killing** (==2016==) followed. The murder of **Qandeel Baloch** by her brother (==2016==) spurred Parliament to close the "forgiveness" loophole in honour killings.

#### iii) State feminism
This phase shows **state feminism** — women's rights advanced through Parliament, commissions and ministries, with women legislators of all parties working with NGOs.

::: fig pyramid | The changing focus of Pakistan's feminist movements.
Welfare and education (1947–1970): APWA
Constitutional equality (1970s)
Legal resistance (1980s): WAF against the Hudood laws
Policy and machinery (1990s–2010s): NGOs, NCSW, new laws
Bodily autonomy and voice (2018–): Aurat March
:::

### 8. New activism (2018 onward)

#### i) The Aurat March
Since ==8 March 2018==, young, urban women have marched against violence, harassment and unequal work, and for ***khawaja sira*** rights and **bodily autonomy**.

#### ii) Digital campaigns
Online outrage after the Lahore motorway gang rape (==2020==) helped produce the **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021**.

#### iii) New laws
The **Islamabad Child Marriage Restraint Act 2025** set the minimum age at ==18==.

### 9. Other movements and actors

#### i) Rural and working women
The **Sindhiani Tehreek** (from ==1982==) mobilised rural Sindhi women; **Lady Health Workers** won regularisation through a Supreme Court order (==2012==); home-based workers won the **Sindh Home-Based Workers Act 2018**.

#### ii) Religious women
The women's wing of the **Jamaat-e-Islami** and **Al-Huda** (==1994==) organised women within a religious framework, sometimes opposing secular feminists.

#### iii) Left and workers' feminism
The **Women Democratic Front** (==2018==) links women's rights with workers' rights, and trade unions of home-based and domestic workers have joined the Aurat March.

#### iv) Artists and lawyers
Poets **Kishwar Naheed** and **Fahmida Riaz**, the theatre group **Tehrik-e-Niswan** (==1979==), and lawyers **Asma Jahangir** and **Hina Jilani** gave the movement voice and legal skill.

### 10. Evaluation

#### i) Strengths
Constitutional and Islamic legitimacy; legal expertise; resilience under martial law; coalitions that produced laws.

#### ii) Weaknesses
Urban, elite leadership; weak enforcement; dependence on donors and the state; backlash and the "Western agenda" label. The different movements — secular and religious, elite and rural — have also found it hard to act together, except on specific laws.

#### iii) The scorecard
The laws have changed faster than lives. Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report ==2026==; women's labour-force participation is ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25).

### 11. Conclusion
Pakistan's feminist movements have moved from **welfare** to **constitutional equality**, from **legal resistance** to **policy reform**, and now to **bodily autonomy and digital voice**. They are many movements — elite and rural, secular and religious, old and young — engaged in one long struggle. Each phase built on the last: APWA's schools produced the educated women who founded WAF; WAF's lawyers drafted the laws of the 2000s; and today's marchers inherit both. Their next task is to turn laws into lived rights for the majority of women.

## Why this answer scores
- A **phase-by-phase history** with exact dates, organisations and laws — true detail.
- The plural "movements" is answered with a heading on **rural, religious, artistic and legal** actors.
- A **pyramid figure** shows the changing focus at a glance.
- A short **evaluation** with current data prevents a purely descriptive answer.
